Setting Up Jenkins Email Notification using Gmail (App Password Method)
Follow these steps exactly, and Jenkins will start sending emails.

📌 Step 1: Create Gmail App Password
Requirements
✔ You must enable 2-Step Verification (mandatory)
✔ You must use the App Password, not your normal password
Steps
Open Gmail ➝ click your profile
Click Manage your Google Account
Go to Security
Turn on ➝ 2-Step Verification
Scroll down ➝ App Passwords
Select:
App: Other
Name: Jenkins
Click Generate
Copy the 16-character password
Example: abcd efgh ijkl mnop
✔ Save this — Jenkins will use this as the password.

📌 Step 2: Install Email Extension Plugin in Jenkins
Open Jenkins Dashboard
Go to Manage Jenkins
Go to Manage Plugins
In Available tab search for:
Email Extension Plugin
Install it
Restart Jenkins (if asked)

📌 Step 3: Configure Jenkins Global Email Settings
Go to:
👉 Manage Jenkins → Configure System
Scroll down to:

A. E-mail Notification (Basic SMTP)
Fill like this:
Field	Value
SMTP server:	smtp.gmail.com
Use SMTP Authentication	✔️
User Name:	your Gmail (example: pranav@gmail.com)
Password:	Your 16-digit App Password
Use SSL	✔️
SMTP Port:	465
Reply-To Address:	your Gmail
Click Test configuration and enter any email.
✔ You should receive a test mail.

B. Extended E-mail Notification (Advanced Email)
Scroll further down to the Extended E-mail Notification section.
Fill these fields:
Field	Value
SMTP Server:	smtp.gmail.com
SMTP Port:	465
Use SSL	✔️
Credentials	Add Gmail + App Password
Default Content Type:	text/html (or default)
No need to touch other settings unless required.

📌 Step 4: Configure Email Notification in Your Jenkins Job
Open Jenkins → Your Job → Configure
Scroll to Post-build Actions
Click Add post-build action
Choose Editable Email Notification
Fill:
Field	Value
Project Recipient List	your email(s) (comma separated)
Content Type	text/plain or text/html
Triggers	
✔ Failure	
✔ Success (optional)	
Click Save

Done!
Now Jenkins will send you an email:
When a build succeeds (if selected)
When a build fails
When a GitHub webhook triggers a build
